{"type": "step", "node_id": 23, "parent_node_id": 22, "role": "assistant", "content": "## Solution\n\nWe need the smallest prime $p$ such that $p + p^{-1} \\equiv 25 \\pmod{143}$, where $p^{-1}$ is the modular inverse of $p$ modulo 143.\n\n**Setup.** Since $143 = 11 \\times 13$, $p$ must be coprime to 143 (so $p \\neq 11, 13$). Multiplying both sides of $p + p^{-1} \\equiv 25$ by $p$:\n\n$$p^2 - 25p + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{143}.$$\n\nBy CRT this splits into:\n- $p^2 - 25p + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11}$, i.e. $p^2 - 3p + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11}$.\n- $p^2 - 25p + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{13}$, i.e. $p^2 + p + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{13}$.\n\n**Mod 11:** Discriminant $9 - 4 = 5$. Since $4^2 = 16 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11}$, the roots are $p \\equiv (3 \\pm 4)\\cdot 2^{-1} \\pmod{11}$, where $2^{-1}\\equiv 6$. This gives $p \\equiv 7\\cdot6 = 42\\equiv 9$ and $p\\equiv (-1)\\cdot6 = -6 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11}$.\n\n**Mod 13:** Discriminant $1 - 4 = -3 \\equiv 10$. Since $6^2 = 36 \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}$, the roots are $p \\equiv (-1 \\pm 6)\\cdot 2^{-1} \\pmod{13}$, where $2^{-1}\\equiv 7$. This gives $p\\equiv 5\\cdot7=35\\equiv 9$ and $p\\equiv (-7)\\cdot7=-49\\equiv 3 \\pmod{13}$.\n\n**CRT combinations** (four residue classes mod 143):\n\n| $p \\bmod 11$ | $p \\bmod 13$ | $p \\bmod 143$ |\n|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n| 5 | 9 | 126 |\n| 5 | 3 | 16 |\n| 9 | 9 | 9 |\n| 9 | 3 | 42 |\n\n**Finding the smallest prime in each class:**\n\n- $p \\equiv 126$: $126$ (even), $269$. Check $269$: not divisible by $2,3,5,7,11,13$ (and $\\sqrt{269}\\approx 16.4$). **269 is prime.**\n- $p \\equiv 42$: $42, 185, 328, 471, 614, 757$ — first prime is $757$.\n- $p \\equiv 9$: $9, 152, 295, 438, 581, 724, 867, 1010, 1153$ — first prime is $1153$.\n- $p \\equiv 16$: $16, 159, 302, \\ldots, 1303$ — first prime is $1303$.\n\nThe smallest prime among all classes is **$p = 269$**.\n\n**Verification.** $269 \\equiv 126 \\pmod{143}$. Compute $126^{-1} \\pmod{143}$:\n- $126 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11}$, and $5^{-1}\\equiv 9\\pmod{11}$.\n- $126 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{13}$, and $9^{-1}\\equiv 3\\pmod{13}$.\n- CRT: $q\\equiv 9\\pmod{11},\\ q\\equiv 3\\pmod{13}$ gives $q\\equiv 42\\pmod{143}$.\n\nSo $p^{-1}\\equiv 42\\pmod{143}$, and\n$$p + p^{-1} \\equiv 126 + 42 = 168 \\equiv 25 \\pmod{143}. \\checkmark$$\n\nThe smallest positive prime frequency is $\\boxed{269}$.\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me solve this problem. We need a prime p such that p + p^{-1} ≡ 25 (mod 143), where p^{-1} is the modular inverse of p mod 143.\n\n143 = 11 × 13.\n\nFirst, p must be coprime to 143, so p ≠ 11, 13.\n\nWe need p + p^{-1} ≡ 25 (mod 143).\n\nMultiply both sides by p: p² + 1 ≡ 25p (mod 143), i.e., p² - 25p + 1 ≡ 0 (mod 143).\n\nSince 143 = 11 × 13, we need:\n- p² - 25p + 1 ≡ 0 (mod 11)\n- p² - 25p + 1 ≡ 0 (mod 13)\n\nMod 11: 25 ≡ 3, so p² - 3p + 1 ≡ 0 (mod 11).\nDiscriminant: 9 - 4 = 5. Need sqrt(5) mod 11. 5 is a QR mod 11? 5^5 mod 11: 5^2=25≡3, 5^4≡9, 5^5≡9·5=45≡1. So 5^5 ≡ 1, meaning 5 is a QR. sqrt(5) mod 11: try 4²=16≡5. Yes! So sqrt(5) ≡ ±4 mod 11.\np ≡ (3 ± 4)/2 mod 11. 2^{-1} mod 11 = 6. (3+4)/2 = 7·6 = 42 ≡ 9. (3-4)/2 = -1·6 = -6 ≡ 5.\nSo p ≡ 9 or 5 (mod 11).\n\nMod 13: 25 ≡ 12, so p² - 12p + 1 ≡ 0 (mod 13), i.e., p² + p + 1 ≡ 0 (mod 13).\nDiscriminant: 1 - 4 = -3 ≡ 10. Need sqrt(10) mod 13. 10^6 mod 13: 10²=100≡9, 10³≡90≡12, 10^6≡12²=144≡1. So 10 is QR. sqrt(10) mod 13: try 6²=36≡10. Yes! So sqrt(10) ≡ ±6 mod 13.\np ≡ (-1 ± 6)/2 mod 13. (-1+6)/2 = 5/2 = 5·7 = 35 ≡ 9. (-1-6)/2 = -7/2 = -7·7 = -49 ≡ -49+52 = 3.\nSo p ≡ 9 or 3 (mod 13).\n\nBy CRT, four combinations:\n1. p ≡ 5 (mod 11), p ≡ 9 (mod 13)\n2. p ≡ 5 (mod 11), p ≡ 3 (mod 13)\n3. p ≡ 9 (mod 11), p ≡ 9 (mod 13)\n4. p ≡ 9 (mod 11), p ≡ 3 (mod 13)\n\nLet me solve each.\n\n1. p ≡ 5 (mod 11), p ≡ 9 (mod 13). p = 11k + 5. 11k+5 ≡ 9 (mod 13) → 11k ≡ 4 (mod 13). 11^{-1} mod 13: 11·6=66≡66-65=1, so 6. k ≡ 4·6 = 24 ≡ 11 (mod 13). p = 11·11 + 5 = 126. Check: 126 mod 11 = 5 ✓, 126 mod 13 = 126-117=9 ✓. So p ≡ 126 (mod 143).\n\n2. p ≡ 5 (mod 11), p ≡ 3 (mod 13). p = 11k+5. 11k+5 ≡ 3 (mod 13) → 11k ≡ -2 ≡ 11 (mod 13). k ≡ 11·6 = 66 ≡ 66-65 = 1 (mod 13). p = 11·1+5 = 16. Check: 16 mod 11 = 5 ✓, 16 mod 13 = 3 ✓. So p ≡ 16 (mod 143).\n\n3. p ≡ 9 (mod 11), p ≡ 9 (mod 13). p = 11k+9. 11k+9 ≡ 9 (mod 13) → 11k ≡ 0 (mod 13) → k ≡ 0 (mod 13). p = 9. Check: 9 mod 11 = 9 ✓, 9 mod 13 = 9 ✓. So p ≡ 9 (mod 143).\n\n4. p ≡ 9 (mod 11), p ≡ 3 (mod 13). p = 11k+9. 11k+9 ≡ 3 (mod 13) → 11k ≡ -6 ≡ 7 (mod 13). k ≡ 7·6 = 42 ≡ 42-39 = 3 (mod 13). p = 11·3+9 = 42. Check: 42 mod 11 = 42-33=9 ✓, 42 mod 13 = 42-39=3 ✓. So p ≡ 42 (mod 143).\n\nSo solutions mod 143: p ≡ 9, 16, 42, 126 (mod 143).\n\nNow find smallest prime p in these classes.\n\np ≡ 9: 9 (not prime), 152 (even), 295 (5·59), 438 (even), 581 (7·83), 724, 867=3·289, 1010, 1153... let me check 1153. Actually let me be systematic. p = 9 + 143n.\n\nn=0: 9 = 3², not prime.\nn=1: 152, even.\nn=2: 295 = 5·59.\nn=3: 438, even.\nn=4: 581 = 7·83.\nn=5: 724, even.\nn=6: 867 = 3·289.\nn=7: 1010, even.\nn=8: 1153. Is 1153 prime? sqrt ≈ 34. Check: 1153/7 = 164.7, 1153/11=104.8, 1153/13=88.7, 1153/17=67.8, 1153/19=60.7, 1153/23=50.1, 1153/29=39.8, 1153/31=37.2. 1153 mod 7: 7·164=1148, 1153-1148=5. mod 11: 11·104=1144, 1153-1144=9. mod 13: 13·88=1144, 1153-1144=9. mod 17: 17·67=1139, 1153-1139=14. mod 19: 19·60=1140, 1153-1140=13. mod 23: 23·50=1150, 1153-1150=3. mod 29: 29·39=1131, 1153-1131=22. mod 31: 31·37=1147, 1153-1147=6. So 1153 is prime!\n\np ≡ 16: 16 (not prime), 159 = 3·53, 302 (even), 445 = 5·89, 588 (even), 731 = 17·43, 874 (even), 1017 = 3·339, 1160 (even), 1303... Let me check. Actually 16 is not prime. Let me continue.\nn=0: 16, not prime.\nn=1: 159 = 3·53.\nn=2: 302, even.\nn=3: 445 = 5·89.\nn=4: 588, even.\nn=5: 731 = 17·43.\nn=6: 874, even.\nn=7: 1017 = 3·339.\nn=8: 1160, even.\nn=9: 1303. sqrt ≈ 36. 1303 mod 7: 7·186=1302, 1303-1302=1. mod 11: 11·118=1298, 1303-1298=5. mod 13: 13·100=1300, 1303-1300=3. mod 17: 17·76=1292, 1303-1292=11. mod 19: 19·68=1292, 1303-1292=11. mod 23: 23·56=1288, 1303-1288=15. mod 29: 29·44=1276, 1303-1276=27. mod 31: 31·42=1302, 1303-1302=1. mod 37: 37·35=1295, 1303-1295=8. So 1303 is prime!\n\np ≡ 42: 42 (not prime), 185 = 5·37, 328 (even), 471 = 3·157, 614 (even), 757, ...\nn=0: 42, not prime.\nn=1: 185 = 5·37.\nn=2: 328, even.\nn=3: 471 = 3·157.\nn=4: 614, even.\nn=5: 757. sqrt ≈ 27.5. 757 mod 7: 7·108=756, 757-756=1. mod 11: 11·68=748, 757-748=9. mod 13: 13·58=754, 757-754=3. mod 17: 17·44=748, 757-748=9. mod 19: 19·39=741, 757-741=16. mod 23: 23·32=736, 757-736=21. So 757 is prime!\n\np ≡ 126: 126 (even), 269, ...\nn=0: 126, even.\nn=1: 269. sqrt ≈ 16.4. 269 mod 7: 7·38=266, 269-266=3. mod 11: 11·24=264, 269-264=5. mod 13: 13·20=260, 269-260=9. So 269 is prime!\n\nSo candidates: 269, 757, 1153, 1303.\n\nThe smallest is 269.\n\nLet me verify: 269 mod 143 = 269 - 143 = 126. So p ≡ 126 (mod 143). p^{-1} mod 143: need 269·p^{-1} ≡ 1 (mod 143). Since 269 ≡ 126 (mod 143), need 126·q ≡ 1 (mod 143).\n\n126 mod 11 = 126 - 121 = 5. 5^{-1} mod 11 = 9 (5·9=45≡1). \n126 mod 13 = 126 - 117 = 9. 9^{-1} mod 13 = 3 (9·3=27≡1).\nBy CRT, q ≡ 9 (mod 11), q ≡ 3 (mod 13). That's case 4: q ≡ 42 (mod 143).\nSo p^{-1} ≡ 42 (mod 143).\np + p^{-1} ≡ 126 + 42 = 168 ≡ 168 - 143 = 25 (mod 143). ✓\n\n269 is prime (verified above). And it's the smallest prime among all solutions.\n\nWait, let me double check that there's no smaller prime in any class. The classes give smallest primes: 269 (from 126), 757 (from 42), 1153 (from 9), 1303 (from 16). So 269 is indeed smallest.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. I should double-check 269 is prime. 269: not divisible by 2, 3 (2+6+9=17, not div by 3), 5, 7 (269/7=38.4, 7·38=266, rem 3), 11 (rem 5), 13 (rem 9). sqrt(269) ≈ 16.4, so we only need to check up to 16: 2,3,5,7,11,13. All checked. 269 is prime.\n\nThe answer is 269.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1786759209}
